Title:
Winbindex (Windows Binaries Index)

Type:
GitHub Tool

Short Summary (4-8 sentences max):
Winbindex is a GitHub Pages–hosted index of Windows binaries (EXE/DLL/SYS) that provides direct download links to specific versions hosted on Microsoft’s public symbol server (msdl.microsoft.com). It solves the problem of reliably locating and retrieving exact Windows binary builds without needing local installations, ISOs, or ad-hoc symbol server queries. The project maintains a searchable metadata database (in the `gh-pages` branch) and uses Python scripts to regularly parse/update entries based on Windows releases/updates. This is useful for reverse engineers, red teamers, malware analysts, and blue team researchers who need to diff binaries across versions, validate patch changes, or reproduce behavior tied to a specific build. It’s interesting because it operationalizes symbol-server-hosted artifacts into an accessible, versioned corpus for Windows internals research and vulnerability analysis.

Technical Focus:
- Microsoft Public Symbol Server (msdl.microsoft.com) binary retrieval/link generation
- Windows binary/version metadata indexing (EXE/DLL/SYS)
- Automated update ingestion/parsing via Python scripts
- GitHub Pages static hosting + database in `gh-pages` branch
- Cross-version binary diffing and build provenance

Use Cases:
- Retrieve exact Windows system binaries for a given build/update for offline analysis
- Diff patched vs. vulnerable binaries to identify changed functions and potential fixes
- Map file versions to Windows releases for exploit reliability research
- Support malware/EDR evasion research by comparing userland/kernel component changes
- Validate incident response findings by matching hashes/versions to known Windows builds

Keywords:
Winbindex, Windows binaries, msdl.microsoft.com, Microsoft Symbol Server, PDB, PE files, DLL, SYS, EXE, file versioning, build numbers, patch diffing, reverse engineering, Windows internals, GitHub Pages, Python scripts, metadata indexing, binary provenance, Windows updates, kernel drivers, userland modules